WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:08.880
We're going back to fluids,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:10.960
my favourite! We're going to talk about

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.320
the drag equation.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:15.200
This tells us the friction, or the air

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.080
resistance,

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:19.119
i.e the drag, acting on an object

00:00:19.119 --> 00:00:21.039
which is moving through a fluid. Let's

00:00:21.039 --> 00:00:22.720
say our sphere is moving that way, then

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:24.240
as it does as it moves through the air

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.279
the air pushes back

00:00:25.279 --> 00:00:26.960
and it's going to create this frictional

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:28.480
air resistance drag

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.720
force and we actually have an equation

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:31.679
for what that is-

00:00:31.679 --> 00:00:33.120
(Brady: The air being fluid by the way? 
You keep)

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.079
(saying fluid but air is a fluid?)
- Air is a fluid! Right,

00:00:36.079 --> 00:00:39.360
fluids are things that change

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.320
shape to fit the container that they are

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:43.600
inside.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.879
So all gases, all liquids, even 
some things

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.879
that you might think of solids

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:51.440
blend over into fluid. So yes, here we're

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.680
thinking air is definitely a fluid

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:55.680
for the purposes of fluid dynamics in

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:56.719
this particular equation.

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:59.199
So the equation says that the drag force

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:00.079
is equal

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:03.359
to one half times rho, where this

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:06.400
is the fluid density, multiplied by my

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.200
velocity

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.400
squared multiplied by the drag

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:11.520
coefficient

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.640
Cd - so this will depend on your object,

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.320
the shape of your object what it's made

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.640
from lots of things are going to affect
this.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:21.680
Finally A which we call the

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.439
2D projected area. So you can think of

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:26.240
this

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:29.119
as the bit of your object that's kind of

00:01:29.119 --> 00:01:30.000
like hitting

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.159
that wall of air. 
- (Brady: The leading face?)

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:33.200
The leading face, yes.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.600
So if you were - gonna test my artistic

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.119
talents - so if you're like a car

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:38.799
you've really got like the bonnet and then

00:01:38.799 --> 00:01:40.240
kind of it kind of angles up and this

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.280
would be like your windscreen and then

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.159
like the roof,

00:01:42.159 --> 00:01:43.439
and then you'd have your tyres

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:44.880
sticking out. So this is a car coming

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:45.920
towards you,

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.000
this area here is the frontal area

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.600
because this is what the air hits

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.040
as the car is coming towards you. So

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.720
imagine like, in all of these situations

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.399
the frontal area is as the object is

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:56.240
moving towards you what do you see?

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.759
And this is it, we just need to know

00:01:57.759 --> 00:01:59.680
these values and this will give us

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.320
the drag force for a particular object.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:04.159
Also it is important to say that

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:08.000
this will vary depending on the Reynolds

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:08.800
number,

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.000
which is something i've talked about

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:13.040
before. And what we tend to assume here

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.760
is that the flow is turbulent so that

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.959
means you're working here with a

00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:18.959
Reynolds number of about

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:22.000
10 to the 4. That is true for

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.680
dropping a ball off a building, that is

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.120
true for a car driving;

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.959
it's turbulent flow, that's when this

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:28.959
equation is true. If you have

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:30.879
Laminar flow which is a bit more relaxed

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:33.120
chill you're going much more slowly

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.120
then it's a different equation. You get

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.040
what's called a linear drag equation, but

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.800
we're talking about

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.599
turbulent flow quadratic drag, this form.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:42.480
So if I have my

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:44.560
sphere and i've got the air coming at it

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.319
like this then this has a Cd value of

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:48.480
0.47.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.319
If we then compare that to say a cube

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:51.920
this has a drag coefficient

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.360
of 1.05; and then something else that

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.519
perhaps some of you picked up on is the

00:02:57.519 --> 00:02:59.280
orientation here is going to be
important.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.760
So if we change the cube and had it same

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:02.640
size,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.879
put it on its side like this, and then

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:06.080
you have the air coming in.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:08.080
It makes it more aerodynamic, the air is

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.400
going to hit and kind of pass around

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.599
that shape; so here the drag coefficient
goes down.

00:03:13.599 --> 00:03:15.360
There's less drag because it's a more

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.200
streamlined shape.
- (Brady: Tom does the)

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.040
(material the object is made of make a)

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.640
(difference towards drag coefficient?)

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.319
Yes. It will in some contexts. I think

00:03:24.319 --> 00:03:25.920
if you've got a sphere that's, let's say

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:28.400
made from metal versus made from

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.959
um lead, it's not really gonna have a big

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:32.480
effect because it's still the-

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.599
(Brady: But lead is a metal!)
- It's definitely having no

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:36.640
having no effect, okay.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.560
If you've got like a steel sphere versus

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.159
let's say a concrete sphere it's still

00:03:40.159 --> 00:03:41.120
smooth enough

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.640
that the change would hardly have an

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.040
effect. But if you had a

00:03:45.040 --> 00:03:47.120
steel sphere versus, I don't know, a

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.640
sphere made of like candy floss

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.159
that's going to have an effect because

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:51.760
they're actually- there are like holes in

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.120
the candy floss; or if it's like made of

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:53.840
like a mesh,

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:55.040
there are holes and that's what's going

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.640
to have an effect. Or even- it's more the
the

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:00.159
smoothness of the surface. So like a golf

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:00.959
ball

00:04:00.959 --> 00:04:02.560
is a sphere but has a lower drag

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:04.480
coefficient because it has the dimples

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:06.640
to make it more aerodynamic. So with the

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:07.840
angled cube,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.280
just to sort of complete this, it

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.080
actually drops from 1.05 to 0.8.

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:13.920
And then the most streamlined thing, it's

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:15.840
literally called a streamlined body

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.400
or an aerofoil, this has a drag

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.880
coefficient of 0.04.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.560
These things, this shape kind of comes

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.479
out of the the actual

00:04:24.479 --> 00:04:26.400
computation and calculation of how do we

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.000
make this as small as possible.

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.919
So the air kind of comes in, it sort of

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:32.320
separates here and goes around

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.000
like so and then this tail kind of

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.759
causes it to come back together at the
end.

00:04:35.759 --> 00:04:37.840
Rather than it breaking off and creating

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:39.360
all of this turbulence behind it that

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.320
adds to the drag,

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:41.840
so the fact that it can sort of stick

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:43.360
and come back together at the end

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.960
makes it really nice and streamlined. So

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:47.840
what we're going to do is we're going to
take

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.400
some well-known objects and we're going

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.199
to pretend that we are dropping them

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:56.240
through the air, or maybe in the ocean. So

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.600
race one,

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.479
uh we are doing falling objects through

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:01.199
the air.

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:04.960
(Brady: This is a drag race!)
- It's a drag race,

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:06.720
okay it's a drag race yeah we're doing a

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.120
drag race.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.759
Imagine taking a human being and let's

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:12.800
say a bowling ball;

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:14.160
and we're going to drop both of these

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:15.759
out of a plane, something like a normal

00:05:15.759 --> 00:05:17.759
skydive jump let's go with that.

00:05:17.759 --> 00:05:18.800
So we're going to drop both of these

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:21.039
objects out of the plane and then the

00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:22.720
drag equation

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.000
will tell you who will win the drag race,

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.759
like which object will hit the ground

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:29.360
first between the human

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:31.840
and the bowling ball if dropped
from a plane.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:34.560
So we need to figure out these uh

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.600
parameters;

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.479
but before we do that we also need to

00:05:38.479 --> 00:05:40.160
think about

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.919
what other forces are actually

00:05:43.919 --> 00:05:46.800
present in this situation. So we've

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:48.160
talked about

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.880
drag, so if this is my bowling ball which

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:51.680
is falling

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.680
as this is coming down the page towards

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.600
the ground the drag force

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:58.800
is acting in that direction, it's pushing 
it up.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.160
The reason it's falling in the first

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:03.120
place is from gravity,

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:04.319
it's pulling it, gravitational

00:06:04.319 --> 00:06:06.639
acceleration. So the force there

00:06:06.639 --> 00:06:10.319
is mass times gravitational acceleration.

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:12.319
So you've got a force balance and this

00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:14.400
will be the same for the human.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.800
And so what's going to happen is when

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:17.600
you drop

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.199
your object, you're initially at zero so

00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:22.319
we're going to start

00:06:22.319 --> 00:06:24.560
start from rest that is important. So

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:25.759
we're starting from rest so we're

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:27.120
jumping out of a plane or dropping out

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.000
of a plane.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.160
So we're starting at zero velocity. So at

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.360
the beginning

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:33.199
you're going to accelerate, gravity is

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:34.319
pulling you to the earth you're going to

00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:35.039
speed up.

00:06:35.039 --> 00:06:38.639
As you speed up velocity goes up, drag

00:06:38.639 --> 00:06:40.960
looks like velocity squared. So as v goes

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:42.080
up drag

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:43.600
goes up. You're going to speed up more

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:45.919
and more from gravity and you're going
to

00:06:45.919 --> 00:06:48.080
have more and more drag pushing against

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.080
you and eventually

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.080
they're going to be perfectly balanced.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:53.120
You will not stop,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:54.740
you will reach a constant speed.

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.880
(Brady: You won't just float in the air?)

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.120
That'd be great, wouldn't it? 
So you accelerate most at the

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.360
beginning and your acceleration slows

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:03.120
down, but your speed is continually

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.440
increasing until it reaches a flat point

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.199
and that's called your terminal velocity.

00:07:07.199 --> 00:07:09.039
Whichever one has the fastest

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:12.240
terminal velocity will win.
- (Brady: Unless you've)

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:13.680
(dropped from really really low)

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.440
Unless you drop from really low, yeah, no

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.520
you make a good point. Your your velocity

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.440
in general looks something like this;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.039
this is time and this is going to be

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:23.360
v for velocity. And then you can see here,

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:24.880
so this would be your terminal velocity

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.039
your maximum vt.

00:07:27.039 --> 00:07:28.880
So what you see is you you increase faster

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:30.240
faster and then you slow down slow

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.240
down slow down slow down slow down slow
down

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:33.840
and then you'd never get- so this is the

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:35.360
shape that you get and you can solve the

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.199
equations and get this exact shape. So we

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:38.160
actually know the-

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.919
these the time it takes to reach your

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:41.520
terminal velocity on a skydive, because

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:42.639
lots of people do it!

00:07:42.639 --> 00:07:45.280
So after three seconds you're about 50%

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:46.639
of the way there,

00:07:46.639 --> 00:07:49.840
after eight seconds you're about 90%

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:52.400
of the way and after 15 seconds you're

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:53.759
about 99%.

00:07:53.759 --> 00:07:56.240
It's tending towards this as time goes

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.599
to infinity, so you're probably never

00:07:57.599 --> 00:07:59.199
going to exactly reach the terminal

00:07:59.199 --> 00:08:00.080
velocity but

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:01.680
you're going to get so close that we can

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.840
say you are. We're assuming here,

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:05.199
we're reaching it and that's going to be

00:08:05.199 --> 00:08:06.400
our speed and that's going to determine

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:07.599
who wins the race.

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:10.319
So to actually get the equation then

00:08:10.319 --> 00:08:11.759
we've got to solve

00:08:11.759 --> 00:08:14.639
drag force equals gravity. So we want to

00:08:14.639 --> 00:08:15.759
know when these are equal,

00:08:15.759 --> 00:08:18.240
we know the formula for drag force, so we

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.479
just say that's a half

00:08:20.479 --> 00:08:23.840
rho v squared Cd

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:26.960
A equals mg; and this is what we're

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:27.759
interested in,

00:08:27.759 --> 00:08:30.080
right, the velocity is what we care about

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.759
so you rearrange this

00:08:31.759 --> 00:08:35.120
and we're going to get: v equals

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.919
2 mg, now I've got to divide by the rho,

00:08:39.919 --> 00:08:42.240
the drag coefficient the area and

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.200
then I've got a square root.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:44.560
And we take the positive square root

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:47.040
because, well we're falling down, but

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:48.240
we have a- we're taking the positive

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:49.920
value of velocity. So this is going to be

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.519
our terminal velocity

00:08:51.519 --> 00:08:54.080
here. So in order to figure this out for

00:08:54.080 --> 00:08:54.880
our human

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:57.200
and for our bowling ball we need to know

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.240
these values.

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.600
And we just plug them in and whichever

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.680
